\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 {\i0\scaps0\b Figure 1.} Examples of generated neutral point pattern landscapes. From left -right the contrast (\u206?\u180?) is increasing and from bottom-up the continuity (\u206?\u179?) is increasing. The contrast defines of the difference between sparse and dense areas of patches while continuity defines the landscape autocorrelation of patch density.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi360 {\i0\scaps0\b Figure 2.} Shows dispersal kernels for eight European mammal species (dotted lines) and the dispersal kernels used in the model (colored lines).\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi360 {\i0\scaps0\b Figure 3.} The fraction of surviving local populations after the climatic shift has taken place as a function if the species dispersal capability (kernel). The results are shown for two example landscapes: one where the suitable habitats are homogeneously distributed (a) and one where heterogeneously distributed (b). For each landscape type three different climatic scenarios are presented where climate scenario A has the highest and C lowest speed. Darker color of the symbols represents higher population densities in surviving populations.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi360 {\i0\scaps0\b Figure 4.} Shows the densities of the local populations during simulations in homogeneous landscapes; the solid black line shows the average over 1000 (?  Jonatan) replicated landscapes, the outer lines shows standard deviation. Red areas show the time when the climatic shift is occurring, blue areas before and after the shift. First row shows results from simulating climatic scenario A, second scenario B and third scenario C. The different columns show different dispersal patterns (group A-D from left to right).\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs24\sl240\slmult1 \fi360 {\i0\scaps0\b Figure 5.} Shows the densities of the local populations during simulations in heterogeneous landscapes; the solid black line shows the average over 1000 (?  Jonatan) replicated landscapes, the outer lines shows standard deviation. Red areas show the time when the climatic shift is occurring, blue areas before and after the shift. First row shows results from simulating climatic scenario A, second scenario B and third scenario C. The different columns show different dispersal patterns (group A-D from left to right).\par
